course: dictionary
lesson: 7547
title: Choosing and preferring
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To prefer something means to like one thing more than another. If you can choose, you pick the one you prefer. «Would you prefer juice or milk?» asks which one you like more, and you can answer with just one of them.
explain: Prefer often compares two things: «Goats prefer leaves, twigs and shrubs to grass.» The little word to shows what is being compared. Prefer also takes a second verb, as in «I'd prefer to walk.» Its forms are prefer, prefers, preferred and preferring.
explain: Prefer is also a polite word. «I'd prefer not to» is a gentle way to say no. Compare «I don't want to» with «I'd prefer not to»: the second sounds kinder and more thoughtful, so use it when you want to be polite.
fact: To prefer means to like one thing more than another, or to choose it if you can.
fact: «I would prefer…» is a polite way to say what you want.
fact: Prefer has four forms: prefer, prefers, preferred and preferring.
fact: Use «prefer A to B» to compare two things, such as «prefer tea to juice».
fact: Animals can prefer certain places: woodlice prefer damp, dark places.
exercise: «Goats prefer leaves to grass.» Which small word links the two things being compared? | goats | *to | prefer | leaves
exercise: Ella's friend says «I'd prefer not to» when asked to play a game. What does she mean? | She is very angry and shouts | She has already played the game | *She politely does not want to | She is very hungry
exercise: «Yesterday Sam ___ the blue cup to the red one.» Which word fits the gap? | preferring | prefer | prefers | *preferred
test: A class survey asks, «Which sport do you prefer?» What does prefer mean in this question? | *Like best | Find | Hate | Forget
test: Which sentence uses prefer correctly? | I prefered tea to juice | *I would prefer tea to juice | I would prefer tea than juice | Prefer I tea would juice
exam: Which sentence is the most polite way to ask for something else instead? | Nope, not that one | Give me the other one | I want the other one now | *I would prefer the other one, please
